        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Polymeric Sand Washing Out",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/why-is-my-polymeric-sand-washing-out/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is my polymeric sand washing out?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Almost always one of four things: it never cured properly, water is being channelled across those joints by a drainage problem, the joints were too shallow to develop grip, or the patio lacks edge restraint and is spreading. Properly cured polymeric sand in a well-drained installation does not wash away.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I know if my polymeric sand cured properly?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Press a screwdriver or a key into a joint. Cured sand resists and feels like a firm solid; failed sand crumbles to loose grains under light pressure. If it crumbles across the installation you have a curing failure and the sand needs removing and reinstalling.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I just add more polymeric sand on top?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not over failed sand. Fresh sand swept onto unbound material cures as a thin crust over loose sand beneath and fails in the next storm. Remove everything loose first. Topping up is only appropriate on sound joints that have eroded gradually over years.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does rain wash out polymeric sand?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Rain within the first 24 hours will wash out uncured sand wholesale, which is why the forecast matters as much as the technique. Once fully cured, normal rain does not remove it — persistent washout after curing points to a drainage problem channelling concentrated water across those joints.",
