    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Polymeric sand that has cured properly is a flexible solid bonded into the joint. It does not migrate in rain and it does not run down the driveway. If yours is washing out, the polymer never did its job — and the reason it did not is what determines the fix.
        </p>

        <div id="diagnose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. First, Diagnose Which Failure You Have
          </h2>

          <p>
            Washout has several distinct causes with different fixes, and the pattern of where and how the sand is leaving tells you which one you are dealing with.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You Observe</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely Cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fix</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sand loose everywhere, never hardened</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Curing failure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove and reinstall into dry joints</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Washout only in one channel or low spot</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drainage carrying water across joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fix the drainage, then repair joints</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sand gone from a shallow surface layer only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Insufficient joint depth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excavate deeper, refill properly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Edges of the patio emptying first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No edge restraint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Install edging, then refill</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Washout after pressure washing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mechanical removal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Refill; avoid pressure washing joints</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gradual loss over several years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Normal erosion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Top up as routine maintenance</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Quick Test</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Press a screwdriver into a joint.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the sand crumbles to loose grains under light pressure, it never cured and the whole installation needs redoing. If it resists and feels like a firm solid, the sand is fine and you are looking at a drainage or depth problem in specific areas — which is a much smaller repair.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="curing-failure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Cause One: The Sand Never Cured
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the most common reason for wholesale washout, and it is unfortunately the one that requires the most work to put right. Polymeric sand binds when water activates the polymer through the full depth of the joint. Several things prevent that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Too Little Water</h3>

          <p>
            The most frequent cause. A light watering wets the top of the joint and activates the polymer there, creating a thin crust over dry, unbound sand beneath. The crust looks like success. The first heavy rain breaks through it and carries out everything underneath. Joints need saturating through their full depth, in several light passes rather than one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Too Much Water, Too Fast</h3>

          <p>
            The opposite error produces a similar outcome. A heavy flow or a pressure nozzle washes sand and binder out of the joint before either can react, leaving under-filled joints with the polymer distributed onto the paver surface rather than through the sand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Installed Into Damp Joints</h3>

          <p>
            Moisture present when the sand goes in starts the reaction early, so the polymer partially sets in clumps while you are still sweeping. Those clumps never bond to their neighbours and the joint has no continuous structure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cured Too Cold, or Rained On</h3>

          <p>
            Most polymeric sands need temperatures above roughly 50°F to cure properly. Below that the reaction stalls. Rain within the first 24 hours is equally destructive — it washes out uncured material wholesale, which is why the forecast matters as much as the technique.
          </p>

          <p>
            In all four cases the remedy is the same and there is no shortcut: remove the failed sand from the joints entirely, let everything dry thoroughly, and reinstall correctly. Sweeping fresh polymeric sand on top of unbound sand produces a thin cured layer over loose material, and it fails again in the next storm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Cause Two: Water Is Running Where It Should Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the sand is sound across most of the patio but consistently disappears from the same run of joints, the problem is not the sand. It is that a volume of water is being channelled across those joints repeatedly, and enough moving water will eventually erode even properly cured polymeric sand.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A downspout discharging onto or near the pavers rather than into a drain or away from the surface.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A patio that slopes toward the house or toward a low point rather than draining evenly away.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Roof runoff dripping in a concentrated line along an eave without guttering.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A settled area that has become a basin, collecting water that then finds a path out through the joints.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Adjacent hardscape or lawn shedding water onto the paver surface in one concentrated place.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The fix has to address the water before it addresses the joints. Extend or redirect downspouts, correct the settled area, or add drainage — and only then repair the affected joints. Refilling joints while the water continues to run across them buys you one season at most, and you will be doing it again next spring.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">A Useful Diagnostic</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Watch the patio during actual rain.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Standing outside in a downpour for two minutes tells you more than any amount of inspection afterwards. You will see immediately where water is arriving, where it is pooling, and which path it takes off the surface. That path is almost always exactly where your joints are failing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="depth-and-edges" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cause Three: Shallow Joints and Missing Edge Restraint
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Insufficient Depth</h3>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand develops its strength partly from bonding to itself and partly from mechanical grip against the paver faces on either side. A shallow fill has very little of the second, so the cured plug sits in the joint as a loose strip rather than being keyed into it, and water lifts it out. Most manufacturers want an inch to an inch and a half of depth minimum.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is particularly common when polymeric sand is swept over old, compacted joint sand. The new material forms a thin cured cap, the old sand beneath stays loose, and the cap eventually breaks and washes away. Reaching proper depth means removing the old sand rather than covering it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No Edge Restraint</h3>

          <p>
            A paver installation without proper edge restraint spreads slowly outward under traffic. As it spreads, the joints widen, and widening joints crack their cured sand and open a path for water. Washout that starts at the perimeter and works inward is usually this rather than a sand problem — the sand is being pulled apart, not washed out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Edge restraint is a structural repair rather than a joint repair, and it needs doing before any joint work. Plastic or aluminium paver edging spiked into the base along the perimeter is the standard solution, and it stops the spreading that is causing the joint failure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Repair Washed-Out Joints Properly
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Establish the cause first</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Press a screwdriver into a sound-looking joint. Crumbling means a curing failure across the installation; firm means a localised drainage, depth or edge problem.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Fix drainage or edge restraint before joints</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Repairing joints while water still runs across them, or while the pavers are still spreading, means doing the work twice.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Remove all loose and failed sand</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A shop vacuum, a joint scraper or a stiff brush. The joint needs to be empty and clean, not topped up over failed material.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Excavate to proper depth</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">At least an inch to an inch and a half. If old compacted sand is limiting depth, that has to come out too.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Let everything dry completely</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Bone dry, which after any washing means a full dry day. Damp joints start the polymer reacting before the sand is in place.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Fill in stages with consolidation</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sweep in, vibrate or tamp, top up, and repeat until the joints hold at about an eighth of an inch below the paver surface.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">7</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Clear the paver faces thoroughly</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Broom, then a leaf blower at a shallow angle. Sand left on the surface cures there and bonds to the stone.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">8</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Water in light passes and watch the runoff</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Shower setting, several passes, saturating the full joint depth. Milky runoff means binder is moving onto the pavers — ease off.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            One judgement call worth making honestly: if more than roughly a third of the joints have failed, removing and reinstalling the whole installation is usually less work and a far better result than patching in sections. Patched areas cure at different times and often differ slightly in colour, and the boundaries between old and new become the next place water finds a path.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Preventing Washout on the Reinstall
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whatever the original cause, the reinstall is your chance to remove every factor at once. These are the conditions under which polymeric sand reliably stays put.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Joints excavated to a genuine inch to inch and a half of depth, with old compacted sand removed rather than covered.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A product rated for your actual joint width, with a wide-joint formulation if your joints exceed the standard range.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Bone dry joints and pavers, meaning a full dry day after any washing or rain.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Air and surface temperatures above roughly 50°F, including the overnight low.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A confirmed dry forecast for at least 24 hours after installation.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Edge restraint installed along every unsupported perimeter so the pavers cannot spread.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Downspouts and roof runoff directed away from the paver surface rather than onto it.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Maintenance Habit That Prevents Recurrence</h3>

          <p>
            Even a perfect installation loses some joint material over the years, and the places it goes first are predictable: the perimeter, any channel where water runs, and wherever a snow shovel or plough blade passes. Catching that early is trivial and ignoring it is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Walk the patio each spring and look specifically at those three areas. A joint that has dropped a quarter inch takes a few minutes to top up and re-water. The same joint left for three years is empty to the base, has let water into the bedding layer beneath, and may have allowed the pavers either side to settle — which is a rebuild rather than a top-up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Topping Up Over Failed Sand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sweeping fresh polymeric sand onto joints where the existing sand never cured gives you a thin bound crust over loose material. It looks repaired and it fails in the next storm. Remove the failed sand entirely before installing new — there is no version of this repair that works from the top down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Repairing Joints Before Fixing Drainage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If a downspout or a slope is channelling water across a run of joints, even correctly cured polymeric sand will eventually erode there. Fix where the water goes first. Refilling joints while the water still crosses them buys one season and then you do it again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Watering Only the Surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A light watering activates the polymer at the top of the joint and leaves dry, unbound sand beneath a thin crust. This is the most common curing failure and it is invisible until the first heavy rain breaks the crust. Saturate the joint through its full depth in several light passes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sweeping Polymeric Sand Over Old Joint Sand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The new material cures as a shallow cap while the compacted sand beneath stays loose, so the cap has almost no mechanical grip and lifts out. Reaching the manufacturer’s minimum depth of roughly an inch to an inch and a half means excavating the old sand, not covering it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure Washing Paver Joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pressure washer removes cured polymeric sand from joints with very little effort, and people frequently create the washout they then try to diagnose. Clean pavers with a hose and a broom; if pressure washing is unavoidable, use a wide fan at low pressure and expect to top the joints up afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my polymeric sand washing out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always one of four things: it never cured properly, water is being channelled across those joints by a drainage problem, the joints were too shallow to develop grip, or the patio lacks edge restraint and is spreading. Properly cured polymeric sand in a well-drained installation does not wash away.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my polymeric sand cured properly?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Press a screwdriver or a key into a joint. Cured sand resists and feels like a firm solid; failed sand crumbles to loose grains under light pressure. If it crumbles across the installation you have a curing failure and the sand needs removing and reinstalling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I just add more polymeric sand on top?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not over failed sand. Fresh sand swept onto unbound material cures as a thin crust over loose sand beneath and fails in the next storm. Remove everything loose first. Topping up is only appropriate on sound joints that have eroded gradually over years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does rain wash out polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rain within the first 24 hours will wash out uncured sand wholesale, which is why the forecast matters as much as the technique. Once fully cured, normal rain does not remove it — persistent washout after curing points to a drainage problem channelling concentrated water across those joints.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will polymeric sand wash out if I pressure wash my pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, easily. A pressure washer strips cured polymeric sand from joints with little effort. Clean pavers with a hose and a broom instead. If you must pressure wash, use a wide fan tip at low pressure and plan to top up the joints afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do polymeric sand joints need topping up?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A properly installed patio should hold its joints for years, with gradual erosion at edges, in drainage paths and where snow shovels pass. Inspect annually and top up the joints that have dropped. Needing to refill within a season indicates a curing, drainage or depth failure rather than normal wear.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

