        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Cured polymeric sand does not wash away. If yours is leaving the joints, either the polymer never bound it, or something is running a concentrated volume of water across those particular joints, or the fill was too shallow to grip the paver faces in the first place.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Diagnose before you buy anything. Press a screwdriver into a joint that looks sound: crumbling means a curing failure across the whole installation and a full removal and redo; firm means the sand is fine and you have a localised drainage, depth or edge-restraint problem that is far cheaper to fix.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever it is, address the cause before the symptom. Redirect the downspout, correct the settled area, or install the edge restraint first, then repair the joints — removing all failed sand, excavating to a proper inch to inch and a half of depth, and watering in light passes until the joint is saturated through. Topping up over failed sand is the one approach guaranteed to put you back here next spring.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            One habit makes the difference between a patio that holds for a decade and one that needs redoing: walk it every spring and look at the perimeter, the drainage paths and wherever a snow shovel passes. A joint that has dropped a quarter inch is five minutes of work. The same joint ignored for three years has let water into the bedding layer and become a rebuild.
          </p>
        </div>

